  "worked_solution": "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector. To find the velocity vector, we first need to integrate the acceleration vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \u27e80,5\u27e9\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\u27e8\\int 0\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 5\\,\\text{d}t\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8{C}_{1},\\,5t+{C}_{2}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(2)=\u27e81,\\,0\u27e9.$ Substituting $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =\u27e8{C}_{1},\\,5(2)+{C}_{2}\u27e9 \\\\ \u27e81,0\u27e9 & =\u27e8{C}_{1},\\,10+{C}_{2}\u27e9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=1$ and ${C}_{2}=-10.$ Therefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\u27e81,\\,5t-10\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the position vector by integrating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \u27e81,\\,5t-10\u27e9\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\u27e8\\int 1\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (5t-10)\\,\\text{d}t\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8t+{C}_{3},\\,\\frac{5{t}^{2}}{2}-10t+{C}_{4}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(2)=\u27e83,-3\u27e9.$ Substituting $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =\u27e82+{C}_{3},\\,\\frac{5(2)^{2}}{2}-10(2)+{C}_{4}\u27e9 \\\\ \u27e83,-3\u27e9 & =\u27e82+{C}_{3},\\,-10+{C}_{4}\u27e9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{3}=1$ and ${C}_{4}=7.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\u27e8t+1,\\,\\frac{5{t}^{2}}{2}-10t+7\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
